#[navbar]
#[title, center: PACKAGES AND BUILDERS PROGRESS]

#[section, $md:
    This page tracks current implementation status for Moth packages and project builders.
    It also records the important accepted gaps and next extensions for partial package surfaces so
    later work does not need to rediscover them from implementation details.

    The @../ (compiler progress matrix) tracks the core language, compiler, memory work and backend
    implementation. Backend implementation progress belongs there even when a project builder consumes
    that backend. This page tracks builder-owned project behaviour and the packages supplied by Core or
    a project builder.

    Accepted deferred surfaces may appear here. Open design questions and outside-scope mechanisms do
    not. Package acquisition, resolver and package-manager sequencing remains roadmap work rather than
    something this matrix designs.

    ## How to read and edit this page

    - Keep package rows focused on the implemented v1 surface and the most useful accepted next gaps.
    - Update a package row while working on that package so deferred extensions do not need to be
      rediscovered later.
    - Keep project-builder rows separate from backend implementation rows.
    - Put user-facing package and builder explanations in `docs/src/docs/packages/**` and the relevant
      project documentation.
    - Put implementation sequencing and unaccepted proposals in `docs/roadmap/roadmap.md`.
    - Use the status labels from this page's legend. Put backend-specific or deliberately deferred nuance
      in the notes column instead of inventing new status labels.
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Status legend

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Status] [: Meaning] ]

        [data:
            [: Supported]
            [: Implemented for the current supported surface and expected to compile, type check and run through its advertised builder/runtime path.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Partial]
            [: A useful subset is implemented, but important common operations, package surfaces or builder behaviour remain incomplete.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Experimental]
            [: Present in the repository, but not part of the Alpha stability promise.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Deferred]
            [: Accepted or design-compatible follow-up work that is not currently implemented.]
        ]
    ]

    ## Coverage legend

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Coverage] [: Meaning] ]

        [data:
            [: Broad]
            [: Multiple focused cases exist, including success and failure coverage.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Targeted]
            [: Dedicated coverage exists, but not across all important edges.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Thin]
            [: Coverage exists indirectly or through only a small number of cases.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: None]
            [: No clear canonical coverage found.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Package model and infrastructure

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Prelude policy]
            [: Supported]
            [: Targeted prelude symbol registration, namespace-alias plumbing and collision rejection coverage.]
            [: Frontend / JS]
            [: The builtin prelude policy registers [$html:<code>io</code>] as a namespace alias to [$html:<code>@core/io</code>] and leaves compiler-owned [$html:<code>Error</code>] as a bare language symbol. Prelude is visibility policy, not a package origin or backing. There is no [$html:<code>@core/prelude</code>] package.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Binding-backed platform packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad package dependency, external constant, external signature, provider-created JS package, runtime asset/glue, Core package, prelude and backend validation coverage.]
            [: Frontend / JS]
            [: Rust-side and provider-created binding-backed packages expose free functions, opaque types and compile-time scalar constants. Project-local and builder-owned annotated [$html:<code>.js</code>] files can create JS-backed package surfaces. Binding-backed packages do not expose receiver methods. Unsupported reachable calls for an active backend fail before lowering.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Source-backed package roots]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted AST-backed config loading, known-key and shape enforcement, self-contained config dependency rejection, generic package-root discovery, collision detection and public-surface enforcement coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML]
            [: Source-backed packages and regular modules share module-root and visibility rules. [$html:<code>config.moth</code>] is compiled as one self-contained source before package discovery and rejects every dependency clause before path resolution.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder-provided source-backed packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted builder source-backed package root registration and HTML Builder package coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML]
            [: Builders expose source-backed packages through [$html:<code>BuilderSurface.source_packages</code>]. The HTML builder provides [$html:<code>@html</code>] as a source-backed package. Builder and project-local prefix collisions are hard config errors.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Project-local packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted structural support-package roots, rejected legacy package-folder configuration, independently registered builder packages, dependency resolution, generic normal module-root and public-surface coverage.]
            [: Frontend / HTML]
            [: Structural [$html:<code>+*.moth</code>] support packages and the optional project-root facade define the accepted project-local package model. Legacy config-driven [$html:<code>package_folders</code>] paths and the historical default [$html:<code>lib/</code>] are removed. Independently registered Builder/Core source packages retain their own package boundaries.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder runtime packages]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted built-in JS-backed package, runtime asset, runtime module, import-map, glue and backend validation coverage.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: Builder-owned runtime packages such as [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>] are binding-backed packages. They share the specialised external package registry and HTML external JS asset/glue pipeline with provider-created imports.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: First-party no-third-party-dependencies guard]
            [: Supported]
            [: Targeted package-manager, vendored-root, allowed-runtime-import and rejected-bare-import coverage.]
            [: Validation / packages]
            [: The [$html:<code>first-party-deps</code>] validation owner inspects only first-party implementation roots and the inline Moth runtime source. User-owned and future dependency packages, generated glue, documentation, tests and benchmarks remain outside its scan.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Core packages

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes and accepted next gaps] ]

        [data:
            [: Core IO package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Console helpers, prelude/explicit namespace dependencies, old callable rejection, removed public IO, input metadata/type-checking, HTML-JS input helper emission and backend unsupported-call diagnostics.]
            [: Frontend / JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/io</code>] exposes [$html:<code>io.print</code>], [$html:<code>io.line</code>], [$html:<code>io.debug</code>], [$html:<code>io.warn</code>], [$html:<code>io.error</code>] and [$html:<code>io.input.*</code>]. The prelude injects [$html:<code>io</code>] as a compile-time namespace alias, not a runtime value. Its future scope and the surface that should remain permanently preluded require a dedicated design pass before major expansion. Filesystem, network, timers, event queues and targeted input sources remain candidate inputs to that review rather than pre-assigned additions or separate packages.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core collections package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Broad literal, fixed/growable mutation, indexing, fallibility, AST/HIR call target, JS helper and diagnostic coverage.]
            [: Frontend / JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/collections</code>] provides the compiler-owned collection operation identities behind fixed and growable collection members. Common non-sorting gaps still need a focused package audit. The accepted stable-by-default sorting contract is owned by its specialised package plan and remains blocked on final mixed-backend collection support.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core math package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted direct, aliased, namespace, const, arity, type, missing-symbol, unbound access, finite-result boundary and HTML-Wasm unsupported-path coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML-Wasm validation]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/math</code>] exposes Float math functions and scalar constants. It requires an explicit dependency clause. HTML-JS routes every single-Float external result through the shared finite-result boundary, rejecting NaN and both infinities before ordinary Moth code observes them. Common mathematical gaps should be filled before expanding into specialised APIs.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core text package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted runtime, JS helper, arity, type-error, edge-case, unsupported-builder and Wasm-unsupported coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/text</code>] exposes string helpers such as length, emptiness, containment, prefix and suffix checks. HTML-JS [$html:<code>text.length</code>] counts Unicode scalar values with the same unit as [$html:<code>Char</code>]. Common Unicode-aware inspection, search and transformation gaps remain the priority for later slices.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core random package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted dependency, JS helper, runtime smoke, arity, type-error and Wasm-unsupported coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/random</code>] exposes random Float and inclusive random Int helpers. Seeded random and additional common distributions remain deferred. Exact generator algorithm identity is not currently a cross-backend semantic promise, so JS may use a backend-local implementation while portable observable contracts remain stable.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Core time package]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted dependency, direct alias, namespace, helper, runtime smoke, fallible parse/catch, arity/type diagnostic, old-name rejection, opaque-field rejection and backend validation coverage.]
            [: JS / HTML]
            [: [$html:<code>@core/time</code>] exposes opaque Duration, TimeMark and Timestamp values plus the first JS-backed time helper slice. Calendar, civil-time and time-zone APIs remain future work. Time values, parsing and other deterministic semantics must stay backend-neutral rather than inheriting JavaScript-specific behaviour.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## HTML project builder

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage] [: Runtime target] [: Notes and accepted next gaps] ]

        [data:
            [: HTML project builds]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad routing, API-only artifact filtering, document shell, metadata, config, output cleanup, external JS glue, page-fragment and resource-output coverage.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: This is the release-critical project builder. Only roots with HTML artefact activity participate in page, runtime asset, resource-output and homepage planning. Backend implementation and HTML-Wasm progress remain on the compiler progress matrix.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Moth template [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] content assets]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted compiler-integrated dependency binding, synthetic content constant, markdown folding, module-root re-export, direct API, diagnostics and integration coverage.]
            [: HTML builder / frontend]
            [: [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] files are builder-supported source assets that synthesize one private compile-time [$html:<code>content #String</code>] constant from an implicit [$html:<code>$md</code>] template body. Nested templates without an explicit directive also default to Markdown and explicit directives override that default. They bind extensionlessly into [$html:<code>.moth</code>] and must fully fold.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Plain Markdown [$html:<code>.md</code>] content assets]
            [: Supported]
            [: Broad extensionless dependency, direct alias, module-root re-export, parser options, raw HTML, literal links/images, collision, direct-extension and module-root rejection coverage.]
            [: HTML builder / frontend]
            [: [$html:<code>.md</code>] files render to HTML at compile time and synthesize one private [$html:<code>content #String</code>] constant. They do not parse Moth declarations, interpolation, templates, frontmatter or metadata.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Builder-supported source file kinds]
            [: Partial]
            [: Targeted moth template and Markdown source-kind registry, extensionless resolution, unsupported-kind diagnostics, source discovery and tokenizer/preparation coverage.]
            [: Stage 0 / frontend]
            [: Builders can declare non-[$html:<code>.moth</code>] source file kinds that participate in source dependency discovery and frontend preparation. The HTML builder currently implements [$html:<code>.mtf</code>] and plain Markdown [$html:<code>.md</code>] content assets.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: HTML Builder package [$html:<code>@html</code>]]
            [: Partial]
            [: Source-backed Builder package registration plus docs-site use across templates, style helpers, tables, code blocks and canvas wrappers.]
            [: HTML builder]
            [: [$html:<code>@html</code>] provides compile-time HTML helpers and source-owned wrappers over lower-level builder bindings such as [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>]. It should grow only where helpers are broadly useful to HTML projects rather than becoming a general web framework.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Canvas package [$html:<code>@web/canvas</code>]]
            [: Partial]
            [: Binding-backed package registration, opaque handle types, runtime assets/glue and current drawing API coverage.]
            [: HTML / JS]
            [: The current surface covers canvas element management, 2D context state, rectangles, paths, text, gradients, patterns, image drawing, image data and pixel access. Canvas is deliberately allowed a broader expansion target than most packages because visuals and animation make it a useful Moth language and runtime stress-test surface.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Accepted deferred package and builder work

    [styled_table:
        [header: [: Surface] [: Status] [: Coverage / diagnostic direction] [: Notes] ]

        [data:
            [: Source-backed package HIR caching]
            [: Deferred]
            [: No precompiled source-backed package cache.]
            [: Source-backed packages are compiled from source each build. Caching stays deferred until package identities and the package system are stable.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Package manager]
            [: Deferred]
            [: No package manager, remote registries, lockfiles or dependency resolver.]
            [: Project dependency declarations, resolver/catalog handoff and package-manager foundations are owned by their later roadmap work. This matrix records status only and does not design that system.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Binding-backed JS package expansion]
            [: Deferred]
            [: JS provider diagnostics reject unsupported public binding forms.]
            [: The HTML builder supports annotated single-file [$html:<code>.js</code>] imports and builder-owned JS-backed packages. Additional binding forms should be added only when their package contract is clear and they fit existing package ownership.]
        ]

        [data:
            [: Generic binding-backed package functions]
            [: Deferred]
            [: External signature validation rejects unsupported generic binding-backed surfaces.]
            [: Binding-backed packages currently expose concrete functions, opaque types and constants only. Generic binding-backed functions remain deferred and must not be introduced just to flesh out a package.]
        ]
    ]
]

#[section, $md:
    ## Maintenance rule

    Keep this page concise but useful as a handoff between package iterations.

    - Record the important common missing operations while a package is being worked on.
    - Move implementation sequencing and unaccepted package ideas to `docs/roadmap/roadmap.md`.
    - Keep backend implementation status on @../ (the compiler progress matrix).
    - Keep package API explanations and examples in `docs/src/docs/packages/**`.
    - Add a new package row only after that package's scope and semantic contract have been deliberately considered.
    - Do not invent package origins or tiers to classify importance. Use only the package model accepted by the canonical package documentation.
]
